<%# The app's confirmation dialog, rendered once per layout and driven by Turbo.

    A native <dialog>, unlike `shared/_modal` and `dialog_controller`, which hand-roll an overlay and
    therefore hand-roll a focus trap, an Escape handler and focus return. `showModal()` gives all three
    from the browser, plus the backdrop, plus inertness of the page behind it - so this is a fraction of
    the code and cannot drift from the platform's behaviour. The older two predate that being reliable;
    they are not worth converting today, but a new modal should start here.

    Named by its own message: a confirmation's question *is* its title, so `aria-labelledby` points at
    the question rather than at an invented heading. "Are you sure?" above "Finalize and pay $5.60 to 2
    students?" would be a heading that says less than the body under it.

    **Two parts: the question, then what the action actually does.** A one-line confirmation can only
    restate the button - "Reset Ada's password?" tells a teacher nothing they did not know when they
    clicked - and reported as exactly that: it should say whether the student gets an email, and what
    happens. That is the shape of every confirmation worth reading: macOS pairs message text with
    informative text, and Polaris and GOV.UK both put the consequence in the body. `aria-describedby`
    points at it, so a screen reader gets the consequence with the question rather than after it.

    The body arrives in the same string, after the first blank line, because that is the **only** channel
    available. Turbo copies exactly five attributes onto the form it builds for a link
    (`data-turbo-method`, `-frame`, `-action`, `-confirm`, `-stream`) and passes no submitter for one, so
    a second data attribute on the link would reach nothing. Splitting the message works identically for
    a link, a `button_to` and a helper-generated row action - and if this controller never connects, the
    native `confirm()` renders the newline perfectly well.

    Cancel carries `autofocus` and comes first. The safe option takes focus in a destructive dialog -
    macOS, GitHub and Polaris all do this - so a stray Enter dismisses rather than confirms. It is also
    first in the DOM, which is the reading order the buttons are in.

    The accept button's label is filled in from the control that was pressed, so it reads "Finalize
    grades" rather than "OK". Its text here is only what shows if that lookup finds nothing. %>
<%= tag.dialog id: "confirm-dialog",
               class: "inset-x-4 m-auto w-auto max-w-md rounded-2xl bg-white p-0 shadow-2xl backdrop:bg-black/50",
               aria: { labelledby: "confirm-dialog-message", describedby: "confirm-dialog-body" },
               data: { controller: "confirm-dialog",
                       action: "close->confirm-dialog#closed click->confirm-dialog#backdrop" } do %>
  <div class="p-5">
    <p id="confirm-dialog-message"
       class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900"
       data-confirm-dialog-target="message"></p>

    <%# Hidden until it has something in it, so a confirmation with no consequence to explain does not
        leave a gap under its question. %>
    <p id="confirm-dialog-body"
       class="mt-2 hidden text-sm text-slate-600"
       data-confirm-dialog-target="body"></p>

    <%# Trailing-aligned, which is not the leading edge this document requires of **form** actions. A
        dialog is the other case named in that rule: macOS, Polaris, Material and GitHub all right-align
        a dialog's buttons with the accept last, because the reader has arrived at a question and the
        answer sits where the reading ends. %>
    <div class="mt-5 flex flex-wrap justify-end gap-3">
      <button type="button"
              autofocus
              class="tw-btn-secondary"
              data-action="confirm-dialog#cancel">
        Cancel
      </button>

      <button type="button"
              class="tw-btn-primary"
              data-confirm-dialog-target="accept"
              data-action="confirm-dialog#accept">
        Confirm
      </button>
    </div>
  </div>
<% end %>
